Markdown# Performance Engineering & On-Device Optimization Strategy

Running deep neural translation pipelines (IndicTrans2 200M + MarianMT + IndicXlit) entirely offline on resource-constrained mobile hardware requires aggressive hardware- and operating-system-level optimizations. 

This document details the low-level optimizations implemented to maintain sub-second inference latency, eliminate Out-Of-Memory (OOM) events, and restrict runtime memory consumption to **<350MB peak RAM**.

---

## 1. Zero-Copy In-Place Memory Mapping (`mmap`)

### The Challenge
Standard Android asset handling extracts bundled models from the APK into internal storage (`/data/data/.../files/`). For a ~250MB model, this introduces a 2x storage penalty (250MB compressed inside APK + 250MB extracted on disk) and wastes CPU cycles during initial launch.

### The Optimization
By storing the model assets uncompressed inside the APK container, the native C++ runtime uses memory-mapped I/O (`mmap`) via the Android NDK `AAssetManager`. The operating system maps the physical pages of the APK directly into virtual address space without allocating heap memory or copying bytes.

Standard Flow:  [APK Archive] ──(Disk Copy)──> [Internal Storage] ──(RAM Heap Alloc)──> [Engine]Zero-Copy Flow: [Uncompressed APK] ──(mmap Direct Page Mapping)─────────────────────────> [Engine]
